## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It is a gameplay framework
as much as a runtime: the game is written as subclasses of its classes, a game
instance, a game mode, actors and their components, and the controllers that
drive them, and the engine constructs, ticks, and renders them in a fixed
order. It owns the frame loop and the delta time the game is given, rendering
and the camera, the canvas fit, the input actions the menus and the deflector
are driven by, audio cues and the music beds that loop, asset loading, and the
debug overlay. Read its documentation before you start. This specification
states only what is specific to Kessler.

The logical design field handed to the engine is the stage, and the game
leaves the camera at rest, so world units and the stage's logical units
coincide and every position in this specification is both. Every contact in
Kessler is decided by the polar crossing rules this specification states,
resolved in the simulation's own state.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested
with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain
is fixed and supplied.

