### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, any new modules you add beside it under `src/`, and the assets
under `assets/`, which does not exist yet.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<KesslerState, KesslerDebugApi>`:
`initialize` builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them
together as `[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only
view (`DeepReadonly<KesslerState>`) and returns the next state, advanced
against the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next
state, read-only again, and draws it. The engine holds the state by value and
replaces it with what each `update` returns, so a frame builds the next state
from the current one rather than writing into it, and nothing but `update` and
the debug surface's poses advances the game. As seeded, the module's functions
throw `"not implemented"`. Implement them.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `KesslerState`, the game's whole
state, and `KesslerDebugApi`, the debug and automation surface
`specs/instrumentation.md` fixes. What `KesslerState` carries is yours to
shape, subject to the snapshot that same file fixes.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame.
The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color your game
uses.

