## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own
logical units on a fixed square stage of `1000 x 1000`. The origin `(0, 0)` is
the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases downward. The stage
center is `(500, 500)`, and the planet sits on it.

The game is polar. A position is a radius `r`, the distance in logical units
from the stage center, and an angle `theta` in degrees, and it maps onto the
stage as `x = 500 + r * cos(theta * PI / 180)` and
`y = 500 + r * sin(theta * PI / 180)`. Angles normalize to `[0, 360)`, with
`0` along `+x` and angles increasing toward `+y`. `specs/field.md` fixes the
angular conventions every rule uses.

- The `1000 x 1000` stage is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete
  stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel
  density, filling the window's short side.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the
  runtime alone.

## The tick

The simulation advances in whole ticks, `60` per second, each worth `1 / 60`
seconds of game time.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The delta time your frame loop measures accumulates, whole ticks are consumed,
and the remainder carries into the next frame.
{{else}}
The delta time the engine hands each update accumulates, whole ticks are
consumed, and the remainder carries into the next update.
{{/if}}
A second of game time is sixty ticks however it was divided into frames, and
drawing never advances the simulation.

Every rate in this specification is per second, in logical units or degrees,
and every gameplay duration is a whole count of ticks. `specs/field.md` fixes
the order a tick resolves in.
